1. Constructing the 2 × 2 Non-Hermitian Matrix

Consider the following 2 × 2 matrix Hamiltonian M(s) containing a real parameter s:
\(M(s)=\left(\begin{matrix}1+i&s\\ s&1-i\end{matrix}\right)\)
• Verifying Non-Hermiticity: Taking the Hermitian conjugate of the matrix (transposing it and changing i to -i) gives:
\(M^{\dag }(s)=\left(\begin{matrix}1-i&s\\ s&1+i\end{matrix}\right)\ne M(s)\)
Because +i and -i on the diagonal swap places, this matrix is clearly not Hermitian.
• Verifying PT Symmetry:
	• Let the parity operator be \(P = \sigma_x = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}\) (which swaps the positions of the two energy levels).
	• The time-reversal operator T is an anti-linear operator that takes the complex conjugate of numbers (i → -i).
	• Applying the PT transformation to M(s) (i.e., \(P M^*(s) P^{-1}\)): complex conjugation first turns the diagonal entries into 1-i and 1+i, and then P swaps the two rows and columns, returning the matrix back to the exact original M(s). Thus, it is strictly PT-symmetric.

2. Solving for the Eigenvalues (Energy Spectrum)

Compute the characteristic equation \(\det(M(s) - E I) = 0\):
\(\det \left(\begin{matrix}1+i-E&s\\ s&1-i-E\end{matrix}\right)=0\)
Expanding the determinant:
\(((1-E)+i)((1-E)-i)-s^{2}=0\)
\((1-E)^{2}-i^{2}-s^{2}=0\)
\((1-E)^{2}+1-s^{2}=0\implies (E-1)^{2}=s^{2}-1\)
Solving for the two energy eigenvalues gives:
\(E_{\pm }=1\pm \sqrt{s^{2}-1}\)

3. Three Physical Regimes and "Phase Transitions"

This simple formula captures three distinct physical regimes of a PT-symmetric system:
• Regime 1: Unbroken PT-Symmetric Phase (s > 1)
	• When the coupling strength s > 1, we have s² - 1 > 0, so the square root is real.
	• Result: Both energy eigenvalues \(E_+ = 1 + \sqrt{s^2 - 1}\) and \(E_- = 1 - \sqrt{s^2 - 1}\) are purely real! This proves that a non-Hermitian system can possess completely real physical energy levels as long as the coupling is strong enough to maintain unbroken PT symmetry.
• Regime 2: Broken PT-Symmetric Phase (0 ≤ s < 1)
	• When the coupling weakens to s < 1, we have s² - 1 < 0, turning the square root imaginary.
	• Result: The energies become a complex conjugate pair, \(E_{\pm} = 1 \pm i\sqrt{1 - s^2}\). In optical and quantum experiments, this corresponds to one mode experiencing exponential amplification (Gain, +iγ) while the other experiences exponential decay (Loss, -iγ), maintaining an exact dynamical balance.
• Regime 3: Exceptional Point / Jordan Block (s = 1)
	• When s = 1, the two eigenvalues merge into a single real value E₊ = E₋ = 1.
	• Result: Not only do the energies become degenerate, but the two eigenvectors also collapse into a single vector \(\left(\begin{matrix}i\\ 1\end{matrix}\right)\). The matrix can no longer be diagonalized and reduces to a Jordan canonical form (Jordan block) \(\left(\begin{matrix}1&1\\ 0&1\end{matrix}\right)\). Mannheim points out in the lecture that the graviton propagator (1/k⁴) in pure fourth-order conformal gravity sits precisely at this non-diagonalizable limit, which gives free gravitons zero norm and makes them unobservable in the laboratory.

