Schrödinger equation & Symmetries

The Schrödinger equation is the fundamental dynamical equation of quantum mechanics, and symmetries are the mathematical rules that dictate what remains unchanged as a quantum system evolves. Together, they determine conservation laws, energy spectra, and the behavior of particles and fields.

1. The Schrödinger Equation as a Symmetry Generator

The time-dependent Schrödinger equation is written as:
\(i\hbar \frac{\partial }{\partial t}|{}\psi (t)\rangle =H|{}\psi (t)\rangle \)
• Time-Translation Symmetry: In quantum mechanics, the Hamiltonian \(H\) is the mathematical generator of time translations. If \(H\) does not explicitly depend on time, the system possesses time-translation symmetry, which directly guarantees the conservation of energy.
• Unitary Time Evolution: Integrating the equation produces the time-evolution operator \(U(t) = e^{-iHt/\hbar}\). In standard quantum mechanics, \(H\) is assumed to be Hermitian (\(H = H^\dagger\)), making \(U(t)\) unitary (\(U^\dagger U = I\)). This preserves the total probability \(\langle \psi(t) \vert{} \psi(t) \rangle = 1\) over time.

2. Continuous Symmetries and Conservation Laws (Noether’s Theorem)

Whenever a symmetry operator \(S\) commutes with the Hamiltonian (\([H, S] = 0\)), the physical quantity associated with \(S\) is strictly conserved:
• Spatial Translation Symmetry (\(\mathbf{r} \to \mathbf{r} + \mathbf{a}\)): If the potential \(V(\mathbf{r})\) is uniform across space, the Hamiltonian commutes with the momentum operator \(\mathbf{p} = -i\hbar\nabla\), yielding the conservation of linear momentum.
• Rotational Symmetry: If the potential depends only on distance from the origin (such as the \(1/r\) Coulomb potential in a hydrogen atom), \(H\) commutes with the angular momentum operators \(\mathbf{L}\), yielding the conservation of angular momentum and degenerate energy subshells.
• Global Phase Symmetry (\(\psi \to e^{i\alpha}\psi\)): Multiplying a wavefunction by a constant complex phase leaves probabilities \(\vert{}\psi\vert{}^2\) unchanged. Promoting this to a local symmetry (\(\alpha(x)\)) forces the introduction of gauge fields, giving rise to electromagnetism and the Yang-Mills forces of the Standard Model.

3. Discrete Symmetries: P, T, and C

Unlike smooth shifts or rotations, discrete symmetries flip coordinates or quantum numbers in single steps:
• Parity (\(P\)): Reflects spatial coordinates (\(\mathbf{r} \to -\mathbf{r}\)). In symmetric potentials, eigenstates separate into even and odd parity states, establishing selection rules for atomic transitions.
• Time Reversal (\(T\)): Reverses the direction of time (\(t \to -t\)) and momentum (\(\mathbf{p} \to -\mathbf{p}\)). Because the Schrödinger equation contains \(i \frac{\partial}{\partial t}\), reversing time requires \(T\) to be an anti-linear operator that conjugates complex numbers (\(i \to -i\)).
• Charge Conjugation (\(C\)): Swaps particles with their corresponding antiparticles.

4. Mannheim’s Extension: Conformal and Anti-Linear Symmetries

In the lecture, Philip Mannheim highlights two deeper symmetries that extend beyond the standard textbook Schrödinger framework:
• Anti-Linear (\(PT\)) Symmetry: Standard textbooks require \(H\) to be Hermitian to keep energies real and probabilities conserved. Mannheim shows that anti-linear symmetry (\([H, PT] = 0\)) is the true necessary and sufficient condition for real eigenvalues and unitary evolution, provided the inner product (dual bra space) is defined consistently with the symmetry.
• Conformal (Local Scale) Symmetry: The free Schrödinger equation and massless relativistic equations remain invariant under local rescaling of spacetime distances (\(g_{\mu\nu} \to e^{2\beta(x)}g_{\mu\nu}\)). Mannheim argues that requiring full conformal symmetry forbids bare mass parameters in the fundamental equations, forcing all particle masses to emerge dynamically from the vacuum.
