Both Wigner’s theorem and local phase symmetry (gauge invariance) show how the strict mathematical rules of complex Hilbert spaces dictate the structure of physical laws and fundamental forces.

1. Wigner’s Theorem: Unitary vs. Anti-Unitary Symmetries

In 1931, Eugene Wigner proved a foundational theorem answering a simple question: What mathematical transformations can act on quantum states without changing physical measurement probabilities?
• Preserving Transition Probabilities: In quantum mechanics, the probability of transitioning from state \(\vert{}\phi\rangle\) to state \(\vert{}\psi\rangle\) is given by the squared modulus of their inner product, \(\vert{}\langle \phi \vert{} \psi \rangle\vert{}^2\). A symmetry transformation \(T\) must leave this probability unchanged for all states: \(\vert{}\langle T\phi \vert{} T\psi \rangle\vert{}^2 = \vert{}\langle \phi \vert{} \psi \rangle\vert{}^2\).
• Only Two Possibilities: Wigner proved that any symmetry transformation preserving transition probabilities must fall into one of exactly two categories:
	• Unitary Operators (Linear): Satisfy \(\langle U\phi \vert{} U\psi \rangle = \langle \phi \vert{} \psi \rangle\) and \(U(a\vert{}\psi\rangle + b\vert{}\phi\rangle) = aU\vert{}\psi\rangle + bU\vert{}\phi\rangle\). All continuous symmetries connected to the identity—such as spatial translations, rotations, and phase shifts—as well as spatial parity (\(P\)) are unitary.
	• Anti-Unitary Operators (Anti-Linear): Satisfy \(\langle A\phi \vert{} A\psi \rangle = \langle \phi \vert{} \psi \rangle^* = \langle \psi \vert{} \phi \rangle\) and conjugate complex scalars: \(A(a\vert{}\psi\rangle + b\vert{}\phi\rangle) = a^*A\vert{}\psi\rangle + b^*A\vert{}\phi\rangle\).
• Why Time Reversal Must Be Anti-Unitary: In the Schrödinger equation \(i\hbar \frac{\partial}{\partial t}\vert{}\psi\rangle = H\vert{}\psi\rangle\), reversing time (\(t \to -t\)) flips the sign of \(\frac{\partial }{\partial t}\). If the time-reversal operator \(T\) were unitary, it would turn \(H\) into \(-H\), allowing energies to plunge to \(-\infty \) and making all atoms unstable. Because \(T\) is anti-unitary, it also flips \(i \to -i\), canceling the minus sign from \(\partial / \partial(-t)\) and keeping energy positive. As Philip Mannheim emphasizes in his lecture, Wigner's anti-linear symmetry is the exact mathematical engine that underpins PT-symmetric quantum mechanics.

2. Local Phase Symmetry: Deriving Electromagnetism from the Schrödinger Equation

As Mannheim notes at the beginning of the lecture (around 00:04:38), gauging the imaginary phase \(\alpha \) of a quantum field produces Yang-Mills gauge theories and electromagnetism. You can see this directly in the free Schrödinger equation:
\(i\hbar \frac{\partial \psi }{\partial t}=-\frac{\hbar ^{2}}{2m}\nabla ^{2}\psi \)
• Step 1: Global Phase Invariance
	• Multiply the wavefunction by a constant complex phase: \(\psi(\mathbf{r}, t) \to e^{i\alpha}\psi(\mathbf{r}, t)\), where \(\alpha \) is the same number everywhere in space and time.
	• Because \(e^{i\alpha }\) is a constant, derivatives pass right through it, leaving the Schrödinger equation and all probabilities \(\vert{}\psi\vert{}^2\) completely unchanged.
• Step 2: Promoting \(\alpha \) to a Local Phase \(\alpha(\mathbf{r}, t)\)
	• Relativity forbids instantaneous action at a distance, meaning we should be free to choose a different phase angle \(\alpha(\mathbf{r}, t)\) independently at every point in space and time:
\(\psi (\mathbf{r},t)\rightarrow e^{i\alpha (\mathbf{r},t)}\psi (\mathbf{r},t)\)
	• When you plug this into the Schrödinger equation, the spatial gradient (\(\nabla \)) and time derivative (\(\frac{\partial }{\partial t}\)) hit \(\alpha(\mathbf{r}, t)\) via the product rule, spitting out extra unwanted terms like \(\nabla\alpha\) and \(\frac{\partial \alpha }{\partial t}\). The equation is no longer invariant.
• Step 3: Introducing the Electromagnetic Gauge Fields (\(\mathbf{A}\) and \(\Phi \))
	• To restore symmetry and absorb those extra derivative terms, we replace ordinary derivatives with covariant derivatives by introducing two compensating fields—a vector field \(\mathbf{A}(\mathbf{r}, t)\) and a scalar field \(\Phi(\mathbf{r}, t)\):
\(\nabla \rightarrow \nabla -\frac{iq}{\hbar }\mathbf{A},\qquad \frac{\partial }{\partial t}\rightarrow \frac{\partial }{\partial t}+\frac{iq}{\hbar }\Phi \)
	• Whenever we shift the quantum phase by \(\alpha(\mathbf{r}, t)\), we simultaneously shift these fields by \(\mathbf{A} \to \mathbf{A} + \frac{\hbar}{q}\nabla\alpha\) and \(\Phi \to \Phi - \frac{\hbar}{q}\frac{\partial \alpha}{\partial t}\).
• Step 4: The Emergence of Electromagnetism
	• The extra derivatives of \(\alpha \) cancel out completely, restoring invariance.
	• The compensating fields \(\mathbf{A}\) and \(\Phi \) are none other than the electromagnetic vector potential and scalar potential (which define the magnetic field \(\mathbf{B} = \nabla \times \mathbf{A}\) and electric field \(\mathbf{E} = -\nabla\Phi - \frac{\partial \mathbf{A}}{\partial t}\)). Simply demanding that a quantum particle's complex phase can vary locally forces the electromagnetic force—and the photon—into existence.
