1. How Dirac Took the Square Root of \(E^2 = p^2c^2 + m^2c^4\)

In 1928, Paul Dirac wanted a relativistic quantum wave equation that treated space and time equally (first-order derivatives in both) so that probabilities would remain positive and conserved.
• The Problem with Ordinary Numbers: You cannot write \(\sqrt{p^{2}c^{2}+m^{2}c^{4}}\) as a linear sum \(Ap_x c + Bp_y c + Cp_z c + Dmc^2\) using ordinary numbers, because squaring a sum produces cross-terms like \(2ABp_{x}p_{y}c^{2}\) that do not exist in \(E^2 = p^2c^2 + m^2c^4\).
• Dirac’s Matrix Solution (00:12:51 in the lecture): Dirac realized that \(A, B, C,\) and \(D\) do not have to be numbers—they can be anti-commuting matrices (now written as the four Dirac gamma matrices \(\gamma^0, \gamma^1, \gamma^2, \gamma^3\)).
• The Anti-Commutation Rule: To make every cross-term cancel out upon squaring, the gamma matrices must satisfy the Clifford algebra:
\(\{\gamma ^{\mu },\gamma ^{\nu }\}=\gamma ^{\mu }\gamma ^{\nu }+\gamma ^{\nu }\gamma ^{\mu }=2\eta ^{\mu \nu }I\)
where \(\eta ^{\mu \nu }\) is the flat spacetime metric. Specifically, \(\gamma ^{0}\) squares to \(+1\), \(\gamma^1, \gamma^2, \gamma^3\) square to \(-1\), and any two different matrices anti-commute (\(\gamma^\mu\gamma^\nu = -\gamma^\nu\gamma^\mu\)).
• What Emerged Automatically: Because the smallest matrices that satisfy these rules are \(4 \times 4\) complex matrices, the quantum wavefunction \(\psi \) could no longer be a single number—it had to be a four-component spinor. Requiring this equation to obey Lorentz symmetry automatically revealed:
